00:14:56.240 --> 00:14:57.260
We run the trials

00:14:59.230 --> 00:15:00.745
in Git repos.

00:15:01.960 --> 00:15:02.460
And

00:15:03.970 --> 00:15:04.915
on top of this,

00:15:05.530 --> 00:15:07.480
what we're trying to do now is

00:15:09.970 --> 00:15:13.180
build tables and figures with R

00:15:14.950 --> 00:15:15.745
but also

00:15:16.660 --> 00:15:20.665
now Shiny applications on top
of that. Some of the first

00:15:21.370 --> 00:15:23.890
selling points for
us to really get

00:15:24.565 --> 00:15:27.100
R into the organization was

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:29.710
creating Shiny apps.

00:15:30.490 --> 00:15:31.285
So we had

00:15:32.650 --> 00:15:35.120
a very tedious process of

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:38.170
reviewing pharmacokinetic data,

00:15:39.370 --> 00:15:40.585
where people were like

00:15:41.350 --> 00:15:45.520
entering meetings with a
100-pager PDF document with

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:49.345
like pharmacokinetic curves. And
they had all reviewed them

00:15:51.220 --> 00:15:53.530
before the meeting
with comments.

00:15:53.530 --> 00:15:55.330
And then you would go
through everything.

00:15:55.330 --> 00:15:57.340
And you had modeling outputs.

00:15:57.340 --> 00:16:01.180
And it just seemed like such
an obvious use case for us to

00:16:02.530 --> 00:16:06.760
go into a Shiny app. And
that was one of the first

00:16:06.760 --> 00:16:10.776
thing that we really
sell to our stakeholders.

00:16:12.810 --> 00:16:14.595
And so we

00:16:15.420 --> 00:16:16.200
partially do that.

00:16:16.200 --> 00:16:20.250
But our bread and butter
is creating these data sets.

00:16:20.250 --> 00:16:23.070
It's doing statistical
analysis. It's doing

00:16:24.450 --> 00:16:29.040
summary statistics, descriptive
statistics of adverse events,

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:32.670
like lab results and so on.

00:16:34.590 --> 00:16:38.655
And then at the end, we
wrap all of these things up

00:16:39.540 --> 00:16:42.390
into a submission
dossier, where

00:16:43.170 --> 00:16:46.500
we have a medical writers who
write up documents, send it to

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:47.850
authorities. And now

00:16:48.690 --> 00:16:52.320
there are at least three
health authorities around the world

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:57.180
who require that we send
our data and our code

00:16:57.180 --> 00:16:59.970
to produce both the data
and tables and figures.

00:17:01.290 --> 00:17:04.930
- Cool. Thank you. So did
you basically say to them, hey,

00:17:04.930 --> 00:17:08.550
you don't have to go through
this 100-page document, here's the SAP?

00:17:08.550 --> 00:17:10.319
- Yeah, yeah. I
mean, basically so.

00:17:12.970 --> 00:17:14.274
When you always
have a project--

00:17:15.339 --> 00:17:16.540
when you have a
project like that,

00:17:16.540 --> 00:17:18.609
you always like
tend to maybe go--

00:17:18.609 --> 00:17:21.870
you want to do too much
in the beginning. You want to automate.

00:17:22.450 --> 00:17:25.720
We want to have automatic
minutes based off like the

00:17:25.720 --> 00:17:27.180
comments that
people were doing.

00:17:27.180 --> 00:17:28.919
And, essentially--

00:17:29.620 --> 00:17:32.200
yeah, I mean, in the
beginning, it was just like,

00:17:32.200 --> 00:17:34.180
you don't have to
look at it in PDF.

00:17:35.020 --> 00:17:36.790
We're also [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:36.790 --> 00:17:37.700
here. And

00:17:38.560 --> 00:17:39.940
you can look at
it in the screen.

00:17:39.940 --> 00:17:44.380
And then we'll build out
the modules around communicating in

00:17:44.380 --> 00:17:47.170
the app, seeing what other
people have done, and so on.

00:17:47.170 --> 00:17:49.180
- Cool. Thank you. Travis,

00:17:49.180 --> 00:17:52.780
I see you had a question
in the chat a bit earlier.

00:17:52.780 --> 00:17:54.230
You want to jump in?

00:17:54.230 --> 00:17:54.730
- Sure.

00:17:55.360 --> 00:17:58.930
So Nova's been working on
this open study builder solution,

00:17:58.930 --> 00:18:03.580
which is more focused on data
capture. For those not in

00:18:03.580 --> 00:18:06.520
pharma, it'd be like
electronic data capture.

00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:09.360
So EDC systems is the
front end that faces

00:18:10.060 --> 00:18:13.450
clinicians or their research
assistants or whomever is

00:18:13.450 --> 00:18:16.030
entering data. And
these are dominated by

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:19.045
a couple of giant and--

00:18:21.160 --> 00:18:22.300
I'll just say like--

00:18:22.300 --> 00:18:24.220
let's say, legacy looking

00:18:25.090 --> 00:18:26.350
software providers.

00:18:26.350 --> 00:18:29.230
And so this open study builder
looks really interesting.

00:18:29.230 --> 00:18:34.220
But it's very much kind of Neo4j and
kind of in the weeds on that side.

00:18:34.220 --> 00:18:36.670
Wondering what the connection to
your side of the house looks

00:18:36.670 --> 00:18:39.070
like on the biostats side.
Are they plugging it into R?

00:18:39.070 --> 00:18:41.530
Or do you have good APIs? Or
is that kind of future state?

00:18:42.100 --> 00:18:42.910
- Yeah. So

00:18:44.830 --> 00:18:47.120
just to give a little
bit of history to that.

00:18:47.120 --> 00:18:51.820
So we have had a
system for metadata management for quite

00:18:51.820 --> 00:18:54.895
some years. So whenever
we start up a trial,

00:18:55.750 --> 00:18:59.500
we build the entire trial
design with all of the lab

00:18:59.500 --> 00:19:00.920
parameters and all [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:00.920 --> 00:19:01.730
and so on.

00:19:01.730 --> 00:19:04.990
And that really builds
like a metadata infrastructure for

00:19:04.990 --> 00:19:06.580
when we need to run the trials.

00:19:06.580 --> 00:19:07.750
And we also put in

00:19:09.490 --> 00:19:12.760
metadata to use in
the actual outputs--

00:19:12.760 --> 00:19:15.460
tables and figures and
so on. And we found

00:19:16.210 --> 00:19:19.630
that works really well
when you're just developing our

00:19:19.630 --> 00:19:23.340
small part in biostatistics. But what
we really want to do is--

00:19:24.040 --> 00:19:27.220
let's say I have made a
data about some parameter

00:19:27.220 --> 00:19:28.796
definition. And

00:19:29.590 --> 00:19:30.800
they're writing up a protocol

00:19:31.480 --> 00:19:33.850
where they want to
use that parameter.

00:19:35.620 --> 00:19:37.900
The open study builder
will kind of be

00:19:39.340 --> 00:19:39.950
the one

00:19:40.810 --> 00:19:44.890
source of truth for everything
that's kind of connected to

00:19:44.890 --> 00:19:46.645
data in some way or another.

00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:52.180
And that's why they also build it
on top of this graph technology from

00:19:53.020 --> 00:19:53.520
Neo4j.

00:19:54.790 --> 00:19:56.995
So what we will use it for is,

00:19:58.480 --> 00:20:00.490
like, writing protocols,

00:20:01.420 --> 00:20:03.685
where you have topic-based

00:20:07.030 --> 00:20:08.740
text that you just pull out.

00:20:10.300 --> 00:20:15.910
It's to build a much
more coherent and metadata driven

00:20:18.250 --> 00:20:21.400
full pipeline around the
biostatistics and the

00:20:21.400 --> 00:20:23.395
deliverables we do.

00:20:24.730 --> 00:20:27.820
So we've been using
metadata for many years.

00:20:29.140 --> 00:20:29.640
But

00:20:32.310 --> 00:20:36.150
we want to reuse a lot
of that information in the documents

00:20:36.150 --> 00:20:39.090
that we write such that--

00:20:39.090 --> 00:20:41.955
because a lot of projects today
are kind of making up their--

00:20:42.510 --> 00:20:44.595
not making up their
own. But they're

00:20:45.750 --> 00:20:48.720
slightly changing up the
definition of things when they

00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:49.830
write it in protocols

00:20:50.610 --> 00:20:53.610
or data reviewer's guide or
whatever it is that we're

00:20:53.610 --> 00:20:55.340
handing over to authorities. So

00:20:56.520 --> 00:20:58.980
it's to build a system
to manage all of that.

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:00.675
And for us, yeah, it will be

00:21:01.490 --> 00:21:04.560
API calls internally. We have--

00:21:06.840 --> 00:21:11.010
like the same way we have
a master model of our metadata,

00:21:12.930 --> 00:21:14.460
for instance, STDM,

00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:17.040
study data and analysis data.

00:21:17.830 --> 00:21:21.810
We also have a master model
of all the metadata data sets

00:21:21.810 --> 00:21:25.290
that go into producing both
those data sets but also

00:21:25.290 --> 00:21:27.450
tables, figures,
and listings. So

00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:30.783
we're betting

00:21:31.510 --> 00:21:35.740
really hard on having
a fully metadata driven

00:21:35.740 --> 00:21:37.517
pipeline to

00:21:38.710 --> 00:21:41.440
the extent possible
that we can do it.

00:21:41.440 --> 00:21:42.250
- Thank you.

00:21:42.250 --> 00:21:46.285
I'm going to jump over to a few
of the anonymous questions too. And one was,

00:21:46.930 --> 00:21:50.560
could Ari share a bit about
how he got into the pharma industry?

00:21:51.610 --> 00:21:55.480
- Yeah. So as I said, my
background was in mathematics.

00:21:55.480 --> 00:21:59.860
I was doing a topology and
high dimensional geometry.

00:22:01.570 --> 00:22:02.845
And at some point,

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:07.620
during the masters part of
my education, I was thinking like,

00:22:07.620 --> 00:22:10.365
who needs this out
in the real world?

00:22:11.910 --> 00:22:15.450
So I kind of shifted
towards doing more like statistics

00:22:15.450 --> 00:22:18.450
stuff, doing more programming.

00:22:19.230 --> 00:22:23.700
And I kind of learned that high
dimensional geometry is not

00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:24.720
that different from

00:22:25.860 --> 00:22:30.210
n dimensional feature spaces. And
I got really caught up in

00:22:30.210 --> 00:22:32.540
learning about
machine learning then.

00:22:32.540 --> 00:22:35.100
And I did my master's thesis in

00:22:36.540 --> 00:22:39.300
prediction modeling
on MRI data.

00:22:40.460 --> 00:22:43.450
So I was really intrigued by

00:22:44.410 --> 00:22:47.510
that intersection
between medicine

00:22:48.520 --> 00:22:49.020
and

00:22:49.810 --> 00:22:53.635
technology. What could
you do in that field?

00:22:55.030 --> 00:22:56.428
So I was looking for

00:22:58.060 --> 00:23:01.840
places where I could explore
that more. At that time,

00:23:01.840 --> 00:23:05.470
Siemens were the one creating
all the MRI machines.

00:23:08.680 --> 00:23:12.310
I don't think there's Siemens
here in Denmark, where I live.

00:23:14.860 --> 00:23:19.525
And I ended up applying for
a job at Novo at that time.

00:23:20.770 --> 00:23:21.270
And

00:23:22.150 --> 00:23:25.195
so I started here straight
out of university and

00:23:26.050 --> 00:23:28.420
haven't left
since, I would say.

00:23:28.420 --> 00:23:29.560
- That's great. Thank you.

00:23:31.360 --> 00:23:34.360
OK, let's see.
I'll jump over to--

00:23:34.360 --> 00:23:37.040
Mike, I saw you asked a
question earlier in the chat.

00:23:37.040 --> 00:23:38.200
You want to jump in here next?

00:23:38.860 --> 00:23:41.560
- Need to remind myself
what the question was. Apologies.

00:23:41.560 --> 00:23:44.440
So I saw a really interesting

00:23:45.580 --> 00:23:47.740
presentation from
Novo Nordisk about--

00:23:47.740 --> 00:23:48.240
there's

00:23:49.030 --> 00:23:52.825
essentially a pharmaverse of
R packages that you're using.

00:23:53.920 --> 00:23:56.170
And you have packages
for everything basically,

00:23:56.170 --> 00:23:56.950
which is amazing.

00:23:57.600 --> 00:23:58.100
So

00:23:58.910 --> 00:24:02.220
are those packages
developed like from--

00:24:02.220 --> 00:24:04.160
not from scratch within Novo?

00:24:04.160 --> 00:24:07.290
Or are those kind of Novo
wrappers around other things?

00:24:07.290 --> 00:24:07.790
- So

00:24:08.540 --> 00:24:09.904
they are primarily

00:24:10.670 --> 00:24:12.000
wrappers around

00:24:13.640 --> 00:24:14.510
tidyverse stuff

00:24:15.230 --> 00:24:17.250
because there are internal

00:24:17.930 --> 00:24:21.560
standards to pretty much
anything you could think of

00:24:21.560 --> 00:24:22.880
that there is a standard for.

00:24:24.140 --> 00:24:26.070
And some of them are to

00:24:26.870 --> 00:24:30.980
make it possible to work with
R within our environment,

00:24:30.980 --> 00:24:33.596
like the way we store data,
the way we export things,

00:24:34.550 --> 00:24:37.700
and I said like utility
functions to work with Azure

00:24:37.700 --> 00:24:40.145
DevOps, to work with
Git, to work with

00:24:41.370 --> 00:24:42.945
to all these different systems

00:24:44.370 --> 00:24:48.135
that we have internally.
And some of them--

00:24:49.790 --> 00:24:51.020
can you still
hear me all right?

00:24:52.190 --> 00:24:52.690
- Yes.

00:24:53.300 --> 00:24:55.350
I think it froze for
a second on my end.

00:24:55.350 --> 00:24:57.200
But I could hear
you the whole time.

00:24:57.200 --> 00:24:57.700
- OK.

00:24:59.180 --> 00:25:00.496
So some of it

00:25:02.480 --> 00:25:08.136
is like layering on top of
these metadata to do things smarter.

00:25:09.360 --> 00:25:10.325
Some of it is

00:25:11.690 --> 00:25:15.560
building like a package for
creating tables that are

00:25:15.560 --> 00:25:19.440
specific to the Novo way
of styling tables and so on.

00:25:21.080 --> 00:25:23.060
But one thing that kind of is--

00:25:24.590 --> 00:25:26.870
kind of catch up to us is

00:25:28.070 --> 00:25:30.020
they were developed without

00:25:33.080 --> 00:25:37.430
looking too much to the open
source world. So now when we

00:25:37.430 --> 00:25:38.950
actually want to do more with

00:25:39.650 --> 00:25:43.130
pharmaverse, where multiple
companies are working,

00:25:43.130 --> 00:25:46.505
it's really hard to
separate like the Novo part

00:25:47.480 --> 00:25:51.450
and like the real
functionality of the package.

00:25:51.450 --> 00:25:53.350
So we're working on that now.

00:25:53.940 --> 00:25:57.660
- It's an interesting
design issue then because

00:25:59.070 --> 00:26:02.640
I can understand that building
wrappers on existing things to

00:26:02.640 --> 00:26:06.000
tailor it for your needs is
a really neat way of doing things.

00:26:06.610 --> 00:26:07.330
But as you say,

00:26:07.330 --> 00:26:10.050
it kind of then locks you
into a particular way of working

00:26:10.050 --> 00:26:12.640
that maybe has
problems down the line.

00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:14.220
So there are probably
pros and cons, right?

00:26:14.850 --> 00:26:15.350
- Exactly.

00:26:16.110 --> 00:26:17.360
And we do--

00:26:17.360 --> 00:26:18.960
and I mean, there's also--

00:26:19.620 --> 00:26:21.520
in recent years,

00:26:21.520 --> 00:26:25.620
so many companies have opened up
much more to talk about how

00:26:25.620 --> 00:26:27.460
they're actually
solving things.

00:26:27.460 --> 00:26:29.200
And we're also
doing that as well.

00:26:29.200 --> 00:26:32.970
And we really want to
contribute to kind of keeping

00:26:32.970 --> 00:26:37.270
those lines open. So we
are definitely trying to

00:26:38.190 --> 00:26:40.365
pivot some of our
packages, either

00:26:41.130 --> 00:26:43.830
kind of committing to some
of the open source packages that

00:26:43.830 --> 00:26:47.610
are there or pivoting our
packages and see if they can

00:26:47.610 --> 00:26:50.310
get into the open source well.

00:26:50.310 --> 00:26:51.750
- Amanda, I see you
raised your hand.

00:26:51.750 --> 00:26:52.860
Do you want to jump in here?

00:26:53.820 --> 00:26:58.440
- Hi, yeah. So I'm a
little bit curious about the transition

00:26:59.250 --> 00:27:00.540
Novo Nordisk made.

00:27:02.190 --> 00:27:05.640
I guess there's two parts
to this question. The first is,

00:27:05.640 --> 00:27:11.730
during that trying to prove
that the pharmaverse are using

00:27:11.730 --> 00:27:14.340
R was just as good
as SAS programming,

00:27:15.430 --> 00:27:16.260
did you have--

00:27:16.980 --> 00:27:19.720
I know there's always a
second reader on the data.

00:27:19.720 --> 00:27:22.380
So did you have one person
using R and the other person

00:27:22.380 --> 00:27:24.990
using SAS? Or how did that go?

00:27:24.990 --> 00:27:27.270
And then the other
question I have is--

00:27:29.160 --> 00:27:33.300
I've seen some reports of the
R and SAS data coming back just

00:27:33.300 --> 00:27:34.170
slightly different,

00:27:34.170 --> 00:27:36.840
nothing that would change a
decision or anything but just

00:27:36.840 --> 00:27:37.980
slightly different.

00:27:38.700 --> 00:27:42.900
So how do you sort of address
that as you're sort of mid

00:27:42.900 --> 00:27:44.280
transition here?

00:27:44.280 --> 00:27:48.105
- I mean, to speak to
your first question, so

00:27:49.650 --> 00:27:50.542
our primary

00:27:51.690 --> 00:27:54.302
goal has not been to replace

00:27:55.780 --> 00:28:00.460
SAS with R. I mean, we are
really aiming for a much more

00:28:00.460 --> 00:28:01.990
language agnostic world,

00:28:01.990 --> 00:28:04.930
where programmers use the
language that they're most

00:28:04.930 --> 00:28:05.770
comfortable with.

00:28:06.730 --> 00:28:07.850
In terms of getting

00:28:08.650 --> 00:28:13.150
it into the organization
and having people using it,

00:28:13.150 --> 00:28:14.395
we started out--

00:28:15.040 --> 00:28:18.340
what we really wanted to prove
was that it was possible to

00:28:18.340 --> 00:28:23.500
make a real deliverable with R,
where we made the tables and

00:28:23.500 --> 00:28:24.880
figures in R.

00:28:26.650 --> 00:28:30.646
So there we did the actual
deliverable in R. And we did the

00:28:31.360 --> 00:28:33.797
parallel programming and
stuff to make sure that

00:28:35.170 --> 00:28:40.100
it is that as what we would
expect to get in SAS as well.

00:28:40.100 --> 00:28:43.525
And then down the line,
I mean, I remember seeing

00:28:44.770 --> 00:28:46.060
one of my good peers,

00:28:46.060 --> 00:28:49.270
Michael Rimler from
GSK talking about their

00:28:50.140 --> 00:28:53.230
program called R
for validation,

00:28:53.230 --> 00:28:57.450
where they were rolling out that
everybody was allowed to use R for

00:28:58.570 --> 00:28:59.530
core validation.

00:28:59.530 --> 00:29:00.030
And

00:29:01.050 --> 00:29:01.900
I think

00:29:03.490 --> 00:29:07.640
building on top of that,
we thought that, well, I mean,

00:29:07.640 --> 00:29:09.560
they're already using
it for validation.

00:29:09.560 --> 00:29:14.650
So why not try to use it
in production, like on a very small scale?

00:29:16.720 --> 00:29:18.370
And now today we have

00:29:19.810 --> 00:29:20.845
all kinds of

00:29:21.670 --> 00:29:24.430
mixtures of how people
are approaching it.

00:29:25.570 --> 00:29:28.990
Whether they're doing the
primary program in SAS and R

00:29:31.390 --> 00:29:35.290
is not that important anymore because
we kind of built up an

00:29:35.290 --> 00:29:36.850
environment that supports

00:29:37.660 --> 00:29:40.060
a multilingual execution.

00:29:41.740 --> 00:29:43.570
And now I can't remember
your second question.

00:29:43.570 --> 00:29:44.545
Can you repeat that again?

00:29:45.430 --> 00:29:46.600
- Mostly about how--

00:29:46.600 --> 00:29:50.035
because there are small
differences in the numerical output,

00:29:50.710 --> 00:29:52.250
how is that addressed?

00:29:52.250 --> 00:29:53.460
- Yeah. So

00:29:56.010 --> 00:29:56.620
I mean--

00:29:56.620 --> 00:29:59.130
so we expect that
there is a difference.

00:30:00.310 --> 00:30:02.440
And in many of the

00:30:03.520 --> 00:30:05.590
compare packages
that's out there, you

00:30:05.590 --> 00:30:08.230
can set a criterion on how

00:30:09.610 --> 00:30:13.585
to which decimal places
should the numbers align.

00:30:14.500 --> 00:30:16.105
And there's always like--

00:30:17.090 --> 00:30:20.080
when you're storing
floating point integers,

00:30:20.900 --> 00:30:24.065
there will always be like
a small difference even with

00:30:24.710 --> 00:30:27.123
the same number within
the same system.

00:30:27.810 --> 00:30:32.690
And I think what we
have really been preaching internally is

00:30:32.690 --> 00:30:34.465
that we really try to

00:30:36.890 --> 00:30:39.515
preach conclusions
over numbers.

00:30:40.400 --> 00:30:43.430
So, of course, if you see
something that's totally off,

00:30:44.330 --> 00:30:46.745
you're going to investigate
what's happened there. But

00:30:48.770 --> 00:30:53.030
when we use and set these
two systems up against each other,

00:30:53.030 --> 00:30:56.870
are we still looking at
the same conclusions for

00:30:58.160 --> 00:30:58.660
these

00:30:59.180 --> 00:31:02.450
adverse events or this
lab parameter and so on?

00:31:02.450 --> 00:31:05.180
So that's been our take on it.

00:31:05.180 --> 00:31:05.680
- Thank you.

00:31:07.715 --> 00:31:09.450
Jared, I see you had
asked a question.

00:31:10.850 --> 00:31:14.100
One had a star. So maybe
I'll start reading.

00:31:14.100 --> 00:31:16.250
And if you want to jump in,
I'll look to see if you unmute.

00:31:17.060 --> 00:31:18.170
But Jared said,

00:31:18.170 --> 00:31:21.650
definitely interested in knowing
more about how you pitched

00:31:22.490 --> 00:31:26.150
to your upper management to
use Shiny apps. And Jared said,

00:31:26.150 --> 00:31:29.640
mainly I get pushback because
of validation concerns

00:31:30.380 --> 00:31:34.595
on doing that versus the
standard 200-page PDF files.

00:31:36.480 --> 00:31:36.980
- Yeah.

00:31:39.150 --> 00:31:39.765
I think

00:31:41.610 --> 00:31:42.975
I would say, luckily,

00:31:44.730 --> 00:31:48.442
our management have
been very open to--

00:31:50.310 --> 00:31:55.290
it's almost expected that you try
and solve problems in a new

00:31:55.290 --> 00:31:57.180
way. And that's kind
of what we've been

00:31:57.960 --> 00:32:00.765
raised with here, at least
I've been raised with it here.

00:32:02.130 --> 00:32:02.630
So,

00:32:03.330 --> 00:32:06.330
of course, there are a
lot of technical aspects around that.

00:32:06.330 --> 00:32:07.595
But we kind of did

00:32:08.290 --> 00:32:11.490
the same thing that you
would do with maybe another

00:32:11.490 --> 00:32:14.310
innovation product is that you
would talk with the people who

00:32:14.310 --> 00:32:16.245
are supposed to use it. And

00:32:18.590 --> 00:32:21.090
most of them said that it
was a really good idea.

00:32:21.090 --> 00:32:23.540
And then we said
to our managers,

00:32:23.540 --> 00:32:25.900
everybody thinks this
is a great idea, right?

00:32:27.756 --> 00:32:31.880
And we think we have the
skills to build it. And, of course,

00:32:32.660 --> 00:32:35.870
one good thing is it's always
good to apply a little bit of

00:32:35.870 --> 00:32:38.020
action on an idea before you

00:32:38.720 --> 00:32:42.020
go searching for opinions
because opinions can quickly

00:32:42.020 --> 00:32:43.160
hold you back.

00:32:43.940 --> 00:32:46.220
And we had already
applied some actions,

00:32:46.220 --> 00:32:48.920
like built something around it.

00:32:50.150 --> 00:32:54.236
And so you're kind of coming
up with something that they almost--

00:32:56.150 --> 00:32:59.510
at least we're trying to
get into a scenario where they

00:32:59.510 --> 00:33:02.210
can't say no or at least that--

00:33:02.210 --> 00:33:04.660
like, can we test this
out on one or two trials?

00:33:06.290 --> 00:33:08.645
And then you make sure that

00:33:10.970 --> 00:33:13.640
all of the downstream
processes around this--

00:33:13.640 --> 00:33:15.050
I mean, we're still

00:33:15.650 --> 00:33:18.342
in compliance with
standards. We're still able

00:33:19.760 --> 00:33:22.070
to deliver everything
we're supposed to do.

00:33:22.070 --> 00:33:22.570
And

00:33:23.660 --> 00:33:27.365
now, hopefully, people will be
a lot happier. And now also,

00:33:27.920 --> 00:33:29.000
like CO2--

00:33:29.720 --> 00:33:32.020
we really want to save
some trees as well.

00:33:34.130 --> 00:33:35.405
So that's been a major

00:33:36.230 --> 00:33:39.020
selling point for us
in terms of Shiny apps.

00:33:40.160 --> 00:33:41.105
And then also,

00:33:42.260 --> 00:33:46.550
another thing that I think most
companies say today is that

00:33:46.550 --> 00:33:49.310
they really want to be data-driven.

00:33:49.910 --> 00:33:52.820
And if you want to hold people
to their word, I think--

00:33:53.990 --> 00:33:56.450
our community is
probably one of the

00:33:58.250 --> 00:34:02.990
parts of the organization that
have the most enabling power to

00:34:02.990 --> 00:34:08.270
give to other people who
doesn't necessarily have data

00:34:08.270 --> 00:34:09.275
scientist skills.

00:34:10.170 --> 00:34:11.090
And I think that's--

00:34:11.090 --> 00:34:12.889
if you can make somebody--

00:34:13.580 --> 00:34:18.050
a higher up in the organization
realize that you can make so

00:34:18.050 --> 00:34:22.725
many people do their job in a
much more simple way just by going

00:34:24.000 --> 00:34:27.900
into a Shiny app or maybe
even just like looking at automated

00:34:27.900 --> 00:34:32.460
reports on a [INAUDIBLE],
what be it, that you can

00:34:32.460 --> 00:34:35.440
enable so many people
just by doing that.

00:34:37.380 --> 00:34:38.880
So, yeah,

00:34:38.880 --> 00:34:42.659
that's been our selling point of
trying to get all of this

00:34:42.659 --> 00:34:45.000
into our processes.

00:34:45.750 --> 00:34:48.460
- Love that. Thank you. Q,

00:34:48.460 --> 00:34:50.790
I see you had a question in
the chat. Want to jump in here?

