00:38:55.440 --> 00:38:55.500
-

00:38:55.500 --> 00:38:58.590
I want to jump back to the
conversation about packages for

00:38:58.590 --> 00:39:01.230
a second here. I saw there
was a Slido question that said,

00:39:01.770 --> 00:39:04.830
I'm curious from Ari's
experience of writing packages.

00:39:04.830 --> 00:39:08.470
When would it be considered
overkill to create a package?

00:39:08.470 --> 00:39:09.680
And when is it justified?

00:39:10.470 --> 00:39:11.520
- Well, I mean,

00:39:13.900 --> 00:39:15.930
it depends on whether--

00:39:15.930 --> 00:39:17.070
so, of course,

00:39:17.070 --> 00:39:20.370
now I'm sitting in an
organization where you know

00:39:20.370 --> 00:39:24.600
that a lot of people are
doing the same exact thing but maybe

00:39:24.600 --> 00:39:26.410
just a little bit
different than you are.

00:39:26.410 --> 00:39:30.660
So most of the things that
we do every day is something that

00:39:30.660 --> 00:39:32.835
at least 200 other
people are doing.

00:39:34.620 --> 00:39:40.950
And that's really a strong rationale
for just making a package around

00:39:41.640 --> 00:39:46.395
just working specifically with tables or
Git or repos and so on.

00:39:49.800 --> 00:39:53.380
But if you're working for
yourself or in a small group--

00:39:54.840 --> 00:40:00.060
like, my wife is a PhD
student currently and has also done a

00:40:00.060 --> 00:40:01.770
small clinical trial.

00:40:01.770 --> 00:40:05.880
And you kind of quickly learn
that you can actually gain so

00:40:05.880 --> 00:40:11.280
much from setting up functions
for yourself to repeat tasks

00:40:11.280 --> 00:40:12.270
over and over again,

00:40:12.990 --> 00:40:14.730
to read in small data sets,

00:40:14.730 --> 00:40:19.120
or to format something that
you want to output later on.

00:40:21.240 --> 00:40:26.880
So I think there's probably not
a package too small if somebody

00:40:26.880 --> 00:40:30.210
benefits from it and
especially yourself. But

00:40:31.410 --> 00:40:35.145
in our case, it's pretty
easy to know when you're

00:40:35.790 --> 00:40:37.965
supposed to do a
package. Most things--

00:40:39.570 --> 00:40:42.210
and also kind of referring
to the comment before,

00:40:42.210 --> 00:40:45.960
most things end up in a package
because we expect that many

00:40:45.960 --> 00:40:47.520
people will use them, yeah.

00:40:48.310 --> 00:40:49.840
- How would, Ari, actually--

00:40:50.440 --> 00:40:55.360
helping share those packages
across the organization or

00:40:55.360 --> 00:41:00.190
helping share what other people are
doing in different teams so work doesn't,

00:41:01.090 --> 00:41:04.465
I guess, get duplicated? Do
you have community groups?

00:41:05.290 --> 00:41:08.095
- Yeah. So one thing
that was running

00:41:08.800 --> 00:41:12.190
and I think it's still running
is we had this initiative

00:41:12.190 --> 00:41:16.330
called Radar, where
everybody could post ideas

00:41:17.140 --> 00:41:21.520
to not just code
development but process development or,

00:41:22.630 --> 00:41:25.180
like, let's get new coffee
in the coffee machine.

00:41:25.180 --> 00:41:27.650
Or like whenever people start,

00:41:28.630 --> 00:41:30.290
they should get a
water bottle now.

00:41:30.290 --> 00:41:33.025
And so all these
different things.

00:41:34.300 --> 00:41:36.220
In terms of code development,

00:41:36.940 --> 00:41:38.680
moving into Azure DevOps

00:41:39.790 --> 00:41:41.590
it's been a way for us to also,

00:41:42.280 --> 00:41:45.150
first of all, make like
all of that work visible.

00:41:45.820 --> 00:41:46.320
Before,

00:41:47.740 --> 00:41:52.210
I couldn't necessarily see the
code and programs from another

00:41:52.210 --> 00:41:54.220
drug project within Novo.

00:41:54.220 --> 00:41:58.520
And we tried to really open up
and make all of that work visible

00:41:59.200 --> 00:42:02.635
and kind of in a help
yourself way, where

00:42:03.280 --> 00:42:05.630
if I want to see what
other people are doing,

00:42:05.630 --> 00:42:07.540
I can just now see
all of their code.

00:42:07.540 --> 00:42:09.820
And it's the same way with
the package development now.

00:42:10.480 --> 00:42:14.440
All of that development
and organization of development is

00:42:14.440 --> 00:42:18.145
also available for
everybody to see.

00:42:19.360 --> 00:42:22.240
Yeah, so that's the way
we've done it. Yeah.

00:42:23.290 --> 00:42:23.790
- Thank you.

00:42:25.210 --> 00:42:27.090
George, I see you
had asked a question

00:42:27.850 --> 00:42:30.670
a bit earlier about Shiny.
Do you want to jump in?

00:42:31.490 --> 00:42:33.160
And I think the question was,

00:42:34.250 --> 00:42:35.440
do you see--

00:42:35.440 --> 00:42:37.330
let me just jump
back. I think it's,

00:42:37.330 --> 00:42:41.050
do you see Python for Shiny
as something you would use for

00:42:41.050 --> 00:42:43.420
your use case in
the future or now?

00:42:44.590 --> 00:42:45.090
- I think

00:42:46.150 --> 00:42:47.440
there will probably--

00:42:47.440 --> 00:42:51.070
so I'm not that well
versed in Python.

00:42:52.300 --> 00:42:53.750
But I can see--

00:42:55.880 --> 00:42:59.210
I mean, there definitely
