00:43:17.660 --> 00:43:21.200
And you have so much like
interoperability between R and

00:43:21.200 --> 00:43:24.190
Python that there
might be many things

00:43:26.930 --> 00:43:29.060
then where you either use the

00:43:29.840 --> 00:43:34.190
plumber API or reticulate to
use the best thing of those

00:43:34.190 --> 00:43:35.810
languages. So not even--

00:43:35.810 --> 00:43:40.550
if you were to at some point in
the future do a full trial in Python,

00:43:41.570 --> 00:43:45.230
you would maybe just call
some of the R infrastructure

00:43:45.230 --> 00:43:49.700
underneath it because those two
languages are now so easy to

00:43:49.700 --> 00:43:50.645
kind of switch between.

00:43:54.780 --> 00:43:55.280
But

00:43:56.500 --> 00:43:58.920
I love the quote I think--

00:43:58.920 --> 00:44:04.920
and probably I saw Joe Cheng's keynote
again not too long ago about Shiny.

00:44:05.730 --> 00:44:09.660
I think it was last year where
he says like Python was--

00:44:09.660 --> 00:44:12.150
it's the second best
thing for everything.

00:44:13.620 --> 00:44:14.120
And

00:44:15.120 --> 00:44:16.230
it is really

00:44:18.080 --> 00:44:19.460
when you're looking at doing

00:44:20.330 --> 00:44:23.240
production-grade
enterprise level

00:44:24.230 --> 00:44:28.190
apps or assistance that you build
both in R and Shiny and

00:44:28.190 --> 00:44:30.350
also other technologies,

00:44:31.670 --> 00:44:32.555
I think that

00:44:33.290 --> 00:44:36.425
Python will be some of the
things. And that's also what we

00:44:37.190 --> 00:44:38.720
recently hired for,

00:44:38.720 --> 00:44:42.680
that we want to have
Python developers also to influence

00:44:42.680 --> 00:44:45.800
some of the stuff that we're
doing in R that might be even

00:44:45.800 --> 00:44:47.630
better to do in Python. So

00:44:48.320 --> 00:44:50.690
it's definitely
coming, I would say.

00:44:51.680 --> 00:44:52.235
- Thank you.

