one of them is--

00:45:14.750 --> 00:45:16.040
I mean, this is just my

00:45:16.880 --> 00:45:19.082
kind of personal belief in

00:45:21.050 --> 00:45:25.610
if you want to do new
fun stuff that you think will actually

00:45:25.610 --> 00:45:27.695
help somebody at
work as well as--

00:45:28.430 --> 00:45:30.350
I try to do this

00:45:31.040 --> 00:45:32.225
rule where I have

00:45:33.290 --> 00:45:34.535
no side projects

00:45:35.810 --> 00:45:36.310
because

00:45:36.890 --> 00:45:38.330
usually you're doing work.

00:45:38.330 --> 00:45:40.910
And then you have this
side project that you're really

00:45:40.910 --> 00:45:44.105
excited about that you want to
build up some stuff and so on.

00:45:46.040 --> 00:45:51.710
But people know that the
actual work is really what consumes

00:45:51.710 --> 00:45:53.190
all of your time--

00:45:53.190 --> 00:45:54.327
so trying to

00:45:55.520 --> 00:45:57.740
have no side
projects in the way.

00:45:57.740 --> 00:46:00.510
Whereas if you have
a side project, you're trying--

00:46:01.330 --> 00:46:02.955
I'm really trying
hard to make--

00:46:03.510 --> 00:46:06.090
I'm like, this is actually
what I'm supposed to do,

00:46:06.090 --> 00:46:09.480
like the same with this Shiny
app that we were trying to--

00:46:10.380 --> 00:46:11.880
this is how the
process should be.

00:46:11.880 --> 00:46:13.710
This is how we want
to work with it.

00:46:14.460 --> 00:46:16.800
And also now rolling R,

00:46:17.430 --> 00:46:21.750
we wanted to do more in R
development. And we can also see now that

00:46:22.410 --> 00:46:25.980
we can interface to so
many different things that like--

00:46:27.330 --> 00:46:30.660
but the people coming into,
especially my project,

00:46:31.800 --> 00:46:33.480
if I've told them--

00:46:33.480 --> 00:46:36.330
now we're running these
five trials. Everything is [INAUDIBLE].

00:46:36.330 --> 00:46:39.840
And on the side, you'll have to
learn R because in two years' time,

00:46:41.280 --> 00:46:46.320
we're going to do a trial in
R. So I've really tried to use my

00:46:47.100 --> 00:46:51.420
power in that sense that I
really wanted to change their

00:46:51.420 --> 00:46:56.340
environment such that it's not
a side project anymore. Like,

00:46:56.970 --> 00:46:59.590
I want you to learn
this stuff on the job.

00:46:59.590 --> 00:47:02.827
And we'll try our best
to kind of support you in

00:47:04.200 --> 00:47:05.280
doing that because--

00:47:06.350 --> 00:47:07.880
I mean, there's also some

00:47:09.010 --> 00:47:12.610
psychological aspect of
working towards a deadline with

00:47:12.610 --> 00:47:14.980
something you know will
be used by somebody.

00:47:14.980 --> 00:47:17.875
So you try a little harder.

00:47:18.640 --> 00:47:21.250
But it's also just like,
whoa, this is really new and

00:47:21.250 --> 00:47:24.220
exciting. And there are
people here to help me do it.

00:47:24.220 --> 00:47:29.290
And people just grow incredibly
fast if you let their

00:47:29.290 --> 00:47:31.660
side projects be their
main projects really.

00:47:32.920 --> 00:47:35.080
And another thing is like--

00:47:35.860 --> 00:47:37.270
one of the things that

00:47:38.800 --> 00:47:44.020
made the biggest splash in
terms of now we have some new

00:47:44.020 --> 00:47:46.480
technology we could use is

00:47:47.320 --> 00:47:51.370
it's not the Shiny apps. And
it's not like using R and

00:47:51.370 --> 00:47:54.010
processes. I did this--

00:47:54.010 --> 00:47:58.330
so whenever we have a trial
ending and there might be some

00:47:58.330 --> 00:48:00.175
sensitive information or

00:48:01.300 --> 00:48:04.480
confidential information that you
need to keep secret until

00:48:04.480 --> 00:48:06.595
you kind of do a press
release with the results,

00:48:08.050 --> 00:48:13.360
everybody who has access to those
data needs to be an insider list.

00:48:13.360 --> 00:48:16.900
And what we previously done
is like the responsible

00:48:16.900 --> 00:48:20.260
programmer. So the programmer
in my role kind of tracks

00:48:21.520 --> 00:48:23.350
who has access to the data.

00:48:24.500 --> 00:48:27.850
And then we generate a list
that we send to our legal

00:48:27.850 --> 00:48:30.440
department. And then
they put them on inside.

00:48:30.440 --> 00:48:32.770
So it's like this
long chain of--

00:48:32.770 --> 00:48:33.430
and they--

00:48:34.630 --> 00:48:38.690
up to when we do the
release and so on,

00:48:38.690 --> 00:48:41.540
they're asking every
day, maybe twice a day,

00:48:43.640 --> 00:48:46.130
for this list. Like, did
anybody go off of this?

00:48:46.130 --> 00:48:47.765
Did anybody new

00:48:48.380 --> 00:48:49.730
get access to the data?

00:48:50.930 --> 00:48:52.280
And I remember, I

00:48:53.210 --> 00:48:54.680
don't want to do
that every day.

00:48:54.680 --> 00:48:58.670
I don't want to sit and figure
out who has access to the data.

00:48:59.360 --> 00:49:02.420
And, luckily, with
R, we now had--

00:49:02.420 --> 00:49:03.230
we could query

00:49:05.546 --> 00:49:09.320
the server that we had data
on for accesses and just wrapped

00:49:09.320 --> 00:49:12.980
that into a data table
in a markdown script,

00:49:12.980 --> 00:49:14.390
put it on a connect server,

00:49:14.390 --> 00:49:17.930
and then just made it open
for them and made it refresh itself

00:49:17.930 --> 00:49:19.525
every six hours.

00:49:20.270 --> 00:49:20.770
And

00:49:21.620 --> 00:49:24.425
so many people who

00:49:25.040 --> 00:49:27.890
maybe have some technical roles
that are not really touching

00:49:27.890 --> 00:49:28.925
data in that sense--

00:49:30.230 --> 00:49:34.460
you had so many people
now getting automatically on the

00:49:34.460 --> 00:49:37.633
inside of the list that they
may not have been on before.

00:49:38.340 --> 00:49:41.240
And there are so many people
outside of the area that I

00:49:41.240 --> 00:49:42.950
have. And they were
like calling me. Like,

00:49:42.950 --> 00:49:45.240
what is this thing
that I'm now on this?

00:49:45.240 --> 00:49:45.980
And so listen--

00:49:45.980 --> 00:49:47.000
and that's because

00:49:48.608 --> 00:49:50.330
they want to do [INAUDIBLE]

00:49:50.330 --> 00:49:52.940
about having
access to the data.

00:49:52.940 --> 00:49:57.110
But it just creates such a huge
splash that now we were able to

00:49:57.110 --> 00:50:00.680
automate this part of it. And
so many people were kind of

00:50:00.680 --> 00:50:03.470
affected by something
that we have automated.

00:50:04.340 --> 00:50:07.910
That has really like
resonated like far out into

00:50:09.590 --> 00:50:12.245
our stakeholders
that we did that.

00:50:13.550 --> 00:50:17.600
And I mean, we thought it was
pretty cool that so many people now

00:50:18.290 --> 00:50:22.340
like automatically got on the
list. And that's been one of

00:50:22.340 --> 00:50:24.440
the biggest lessons too.

00:50:25.070 --> 00:50:26.720
We automated something, right?

00:50:27.640 --> 00:50:30.890
That actually created
value for others.

00:50:30.890 --> 00:50:32.930
So that was a
pretty cool. Yeah.

00:50:32.930 --> 00:50:33.560
- I love that.

00:50:33.560 --> 00:50:37.430
It's really cool when you get
to see all of that feedback

00:50:37.430 --> 00:50:41.160
from everybody right away.
Like, just yesterday,

00:50:41.160 --> 00:50:44.420
I did something really small
where I added like a download

00:50:44.420 --> 00:50:47.450
CSV to a table that
was in a Quarto doc.

00:50:47.450 --> 00:50:49.220
And I realized right away,

00:50:49.220 --> 00:50:53.120
that made that report so
much more impactful to somebody that

00:50:53.120 --> 00:50:54.260
they could download it.

00:50:55.180 --> 00:50:57.730
Niels, I see you just put
a question here in the chat.

00:50:57.730 --> 00:51:00.580
You want to jump in? And
I can read it. It is,

00:51:00.580 --> 00:51:04.270
what is needed to submit
a Shiny app to a regulatory

00:51:04.270 --> 00:51:07.705
agency? What are the requirements
that they are looking for?

00:51:08.800 --> 00:51:13.180
- So I might not be the
best one to answer this one because

00:51:13.180 --> 00:51:14.940
it's not something
we have done.

00:51:16.000 --> 00:51:21.550
The only thing I know is
that the R consortium working

00:51:21.550 --> 00:51:25.750
group ARPA submission has
some really great people working

00:51:25.750 --> 00:51:29.740
on getting Shiny apps to FDA.

00:51:30.680 --> 00:51:33.370
And there are many
aspects of that. Like,

00:51:34.600 --> 00:51:38.650
how do you read the data into
a Shiny app in a system where you

00:51:38.650 --> 00:51:40.480
don't know where the data is?

00:51:40.480 --> 00:51:43.930
And how do you spin up
something that can run a Shiny app?

00:51:46.960 --> 00:51:49.210
So there is an association for

00:51:50.440 --> 00:51:52.930
statistical programmers
in pharma called Fuse,

00:51:52.930 --> 00:51:55.145
which has had a
working group around

00:51:56.330 --> 00:51:59.390
interactivity and submission
for quite a few years,

00:51:59.390 --> 00:52:00.620
whether we're talking about--

00:52:01.280 --> 00:52:05.300
if everybody is submitting Shiny
apps, like, FDA will never know.

00:52:06.350 --> 00:52:07.750
There's no red

00:52:08.750 --> 00:52:13.020
thread around, this is how
we do data imports in Shiny.

00:52:13.020 --> 00:52:15.845
So if everybody is making
up their own UIs about

00:52:18.070 --> 00:52:19.345
how you analyze data,

00:52:20.955 --> 00:52:25.180
I think FDA will have a
hard time going through all of those Shiny

00:52:25.180 --> 00:52:27.025
apps. So they've been working
a little bit on,

00:52:27.610 --> 00:52:29.980
should we try and set
up some standards around

00:52:30.700 --> 00:52:34.645
submitting something like a
Shiny app to FDA?

00:52:35.830 --> 00:52:37.396
And I think even

00:52:39.340 --> 00:52:41.560
less could even do it--

00:52:41.560 --> 00:52:44.110
like, if you were doing
just like HTML file,

00:52:44.110 --> 00:52:46.750
if you were using
flexdashboard to do stuff,

00:52:49.000 --> 00:52:53.170
I think that could go a really
long way. And I do believe so.

00:52:53.860 --> 00:52:55.240
FDA has a

00:52:55.960 --> 00:52:58.615
file format
specification document

00:52:59.680 --> 00:53:01.420
where all of the
allowed files--

00:53:01.420 --> 00:53:02.685
and I'm pretty sure

00:53:03.550 --> 00:53:08.490
dot HTML is on that one. So
you could get pretty far probably with

00:53:09.490 --> 00:53:11.670
doing something in Quarto

00:53:12.370 --> 00:53:14.410
or Markdown in that way, yeah.

00:53:15.780 --> 00:53:21.150
- So maybe a big question to answer
in a few minutes here. But I see,

00:53:22.590 --> 00:53:24.075
Jared, you had a question on

00:53:24.720 --> 00:53:28.800
validation. If you want
to jump in and ask that?

00:53:28.800 --> 00:53:29.550
AUDIENCE: Yeah, no,

00:53:29.550 --> 00:53:32.430
this is just a kind of a
hot button topic that a lot of

00:53:32.430 --> 00:53:34.590
groups are going into. But

00:53:35.370 --> 00:53:38.670
just how did you guys
approach the issue of

00:53:39.570 --> 00:53:43.545
dual independent programming and
validating your ADaM [INAUDIBLE]

00:53:43.545 --> 00:53:46.920
and ADaM TLF data
sets? Was it more of a

00:53:47.940 --> 00:53:50.120
one side used SAS,
another used R?

00:53:50.120 --> 00:53:55.220
Or if you both used R when
it came to package and environment versioning

00:53:56.040 --> 00:53:57.220
validation to

00:53:58.380 --> 00:54:00.810
provide that full
traceability to the

00:54:00.810 --> 00:54:04.050
[INAUDIBLE], how did you
guys approach that infrastructure

00:54:04.050 --> 00:54:06.090
behind the scenes if you
don't mind me asking?

00:54:06.090 --> 00:54:06.590
- Yeah.

00:54:07.380 --> 00:54:11.115
So one of the things I think
I can mention first is that

00:54:11.940 --> 00:54:15.505
for quite some years, we
have been working with our

00:54:16.180 --> 00:54:19.660
standard operating
procedures and making them

00:54:22.570 --> 00:54:25.630
more easy to work
under. And especially--

00:54:25.630 --> 00:54:27.010
so we have one for--

00:54:27.010 --> 00:54:30.160
we call it the custom
programming SOP,

00:54:30.160 --> 00:54:31.660
Standard Operating Procedure,

00:54:33.490 --> 00:54:37.750
where we talk about the
different revenue levels that we're applying

00:54:39.950 --> 00:54:42.740
to custom programs
as we call them.

00:54:42.740 --> 00:54:44.390
And there we

00:54:45.290 --> 00:54:49.430
kind of introduced a
self review level, peer review

00:54:49.430 --> 00:54:53.060
level, and then double
programming review level,

00:54:54.650 --> 00:54:56.230
most people call it. And

00:54:56.930 --> 00:54:58.400
we're actually trying to do

00:55:00.140 --> 00:55:02.600
less and less
stable programming.

00:55:05.120 --> 00:55:05.620
So

00:55:06.710 --> 00:55:09.020
for many years,
we've kind of been--

00:55:09.860 --> 00:55:14.870
had our ears blown full of
risk based approaches to many things.

00:55:17.000 --> 00:55:17.500
And

00:55:18.560 --> 00:55:19.880
so we've been trying to--

00:55:19.880 --> 00:55:23.960
you double program the
really most essential parts--

00:55:23.960 --> 00:55:27.290
primary analysis, the most
important analysis data sets.

00:55:28.370 --> 00:55:32.225
And then if you step
one level out to--

00:55:34.340 --> 00:55:34.840
most

00:55:35.600 --> 00:55:37.520
people do double programming.

00:55:39.110 --> 00:55:40.430
They have done it in SAS.

00:55:40.430 --> 00:55:42.770
But most people are
doing it in R now.

00:55:42.770 --> 00:55:44.810
We're also doing R versus R.

00:55:45.890 --> 00:55:49.940
And then in terms of doing
those TFL in order to

00:55:49.940 --> 00:55:53.690
encapsulate like what you're doing on
the trial, I mean, we're running

00:55:54.960 --> 00:55:56.480
R-E-N-V, or renv,

00:55:57.110 --> 00:55:59.510
to control like the
local environment.

00:55:59.510 --> 00:56:03.245
And that's also why we need
it to have those trials.

00:56:04.880 --> 00:56:06.395
We needed to add
them use give--

00:56:06.980 --> 00:56:10.130
because we need to track
like that local environment.

00:56:11.210 --> 00:56:11.810
And then

00:56:13.170 --> 00:56:14.865
if you step one further out

00:56:15.750 --> 00:56:18.570
into how are we getting
packages into the

00:56:18.570 --> 00:56:19.700
organization--

00:56:20.460 --> 00:56:25.380
I mean, we use a passive
package manager. And we apply

00:56:25.380 --> 00:56:28.970
the risk based approach
from the R Validation Hub.

00:56:28.970 --> 00:56:31.330
So we do risk
assessment of packages.

00:56:32.760 --> 00:56:34.605
And we have worked--

00:56:35.700 --> 00:56:38.475
or some of my very good
colleagues have worked very hard on

00:56:42.070 --> 00:56:44.310
automating that to some sense.

00:56:44.310 --> 00:56:46.915
I think if you want to
have a really good example

00:56:49.210 --> 00:56:52.310
of how that would
look like is--

00:56:52.310 --> 00:56:55.195
I think it's Aaron
Clark from Biogen

00:56:57.580 --> 00:57:00.640
has created this risk
assessment app which is built

00:57:00.640 --> 00:57:02.570
upon the risk metric package.

00:57:03.250 --> 00:57:05.660
And we use that as
well for our internal

00:57:07.000 --> 00:57:11.770
risk assessments. And then
the environment itself, like

00:57:11.770 --> 00:57:14.680
the workbench and
the package manager,

00:57:14.680 --> 00:57:17.305
we've had some people from IT

00:57:18.040 --> 00:57:22.720
help to bring it up
to a GXP validated environment that

00:57:22.720 --> 00:57:24.260
we can actually run trials in.

00:57:27.130 --> 00:57:28.840
Yeah, I hope that covers it.

00:57:29.380 --> 00:57:32.130
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I
think my biggest issue

00:57:32.950 --> 00:57:37.360
I've encountered is just
trying to allow any sort of

00:57:37.360 --> 00:57:41.110
confidential or patient
sensitive data into a GitHub or

00:57:41.110 --> 00:57:45.850
even if it's a private repo
or something to allow that to be

00:57:45.850 --> 00:57:50.860
moved out of some firewall
locked state. And so then comes

00:57:50.860 --> 00:57:51.850
the question of--

00:57:53.860 --> 00:57:58.570
SVN or what do you use
at a local level to ensure that

00:57:58.570 --> 00:58:02.950
Docker or Kubernetes or what
to containerize the versioning as

00:58:02.950 --> 00:58:06.190
well as the validation of
the packages and everything so that

00:58:06.190 --> 00:58:09.760
it can be built from that?
And then it just becomes--

00:58:09.760 --> 00:58:14.620
it just turns into this mess
of trying to explain and keep

00:58:14.620 --> 00:58:16.390
separate. So if
that makes sense.

00:58:16.390 --> 00:58:17.590
- Yeah, I mean, so

00:58:18.580 --> 00:58:21.040
maybe, luckily, I can say-- I

00:58:21.040 --> 00:58:25.870
mean, we don't use GitHub. So
we have Azure DevOps within the

00:58:25.870 --> 00:58:29.015
firewall. And I think
one of our recent

00:58:30.440 --> 00:58:30.940
GXP

00:58:31.970 --> 00:58:35.330
requirements are that basically
any system that you're like

00:58:35.330 --> 00:58:39.665
interacting with when you're doing trial
has to be within the firewall.

00:58:42.000 --> 00:58:44.405
And in terms of this system--

00:58:46.700 --> 00:58:47.490
so, of course,

00:58:47.490 --> 00:58:50.030
there's a lot of documentation for
the system that you need to

00:58:50.030 --> 00:58:53.750
have in place if somebody
were to inspect the system.

00:58:54.830 --> 00:58:56.410
R system will soon--

00:58:56.910 --> 00:58:58.250
I think actually tomorrow--

00:58:58.250 --> 00:59:01.925
shift to run on Kubernetes.
It's been running on

00:59:03.110 --> 00:59:05.360
like Amazon virtual
machines, I think, for now.

00:59:06.290 --> 00:59:07.940
And everything there is built

00:59:09.440 --> 00:59:10.760
infrastructure as code.

00:59:10.760 --> 00:59:15.440
So we have control over everything
we do there. And as I mentioned,

00:59:16.850 --> 00:59:19.620
the guys from IT are
really ones helping

00:59:20.540 --> 00:59:24.200
with the requirements around
standing up that environment.

00:59:24.200 --> 00:59:28.160
And they also have
inspection experience from other systems

00:59:28.160 --> 00:59:32.630
that we have. So that's been a
huge help in kind of getting it

00:59:32.630 --> 00:59:34.220
to work for us.

00:59:35.640 --> 00:59:36.390
AUDIENCE: No, that's great. RACHAEL

00:59:36.390 --> 00:59:37.090
DEMPSEY: Thank you.

00:59:37.090 --> 00:59:38.520
AUDIENCE: Yeah, thank you.

00:59:39.030 --> 00:59:39.870
- Oh sorry, Jared.

00:59:42.360 --> 00:59:43.470
Sorry if I cut you off.

00:59:43.980 --> 00:59:47.010
But thank you so much,
everybody, for joining today.

00:59:47.010 --> 00:59:49.980
And thank you, Ari, for sharing
your experience with us.

00:59:50.500 --> 00:59:54.000
I wanted to remind everybody before
you log off if you want

00:59:54.000 --> 00:59:57.750
to hear more about the work
they're doing at Novo Nordisk and

00:59:58.500 --> 01:00:03.090
see Ari again presenting, that
event will be on September 12.

01:00:03.090 --> 01:00:07.200
And I see Tyler just shared the
link in the chat right now if

01:00:07.200 --> 01:00:08.115
you want to check it out.

01:00:09.280 --> 01:00:11.970
But, Ari, thank you so
much for joining us today.

01:00:11.970 --> 01:00:14.280
- Yeah, thank you so
much for having me.

01:00:14.280 --> 01:00:15.480
It's been an honor to be here.

